\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Instance scales used in the computational study. Each configuration is replicated over $20$ independent instances, and the processing times are drawn uniformly from $[1,10]$, so that $V\approx 11n$ throughout. The small scale is bounded by the MILP time limit; the medium and large scales are run under both weight settings of Section~\ref {subsub:instances}.}}{28}{table.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:instances}{{1}{28}{Instance scales used in the computational study. Each configuration is replicated over $20$ independent instances, and the processing times are drawn uniformly from $[1,10]$, so that $V\approx 11n$ throughout. The small scale is bounded by the MILP time limit; the medium and large scales are run under both weight settings of Section~\ref {subsub:instances}}{table.1}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Pilot test of the exact methods on the largest configuration of the small scale ($n=60$, $K=2$), averaged over five instances. The gap is \eqref  {eq:gap} against the MILP model, which is the baseline at this scale and therefore reads zero by construction; a negative entry marks a method that returned a better schedule than the model. MILP is given a time limit of $30$\,s, which suffices to prove optimality on one instance only.}}{29}{table.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:pilot}{{2}{29}{Pilot test of the exact methods on the largest configuration of the small scale ($n=60$, $K=2$), averaged over five instances. The gap is \eqref {eq:gap} against the MILP model, which is the baseline at this scale and therefore reads zero by construction; a negative entry marks a method that returned a better schedule than the model. MILP is given a time limit of $30$\,s, which suffices to prove optimality on one instance only}{table.2}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Pilot test of the heuristics at the lower end of the large scale ($n=500$, all weights distinct and independent of the processing times), averaged over five instances. The gap is \eqref  {eq:gap} against the 2-approximation, which is the baseline at this scale and therefore reads zero by construction; a negative entry marks a method that improved on the baseline.}}{29}{table.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:pilot-large}{{3}{29}{Pilot test of the heuristics at the lower end of the large scale ($n=500$, all weights distinct and independent of the processing times), averaged over five instances. The gap is \eqref {eq:gap} against the 2-approximation, which is the baseline at this scale and therefore reads zero by construction; a negative entry marks a method that improved on the baseline}{table.3}{}}
